Plan:
\f1\fs48 \
Phase 2: Sharding via Consistent Hashing \'97 Step-by-Step Guide\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 Great work finishing Phase 1! You now have a single node that's thread-safe and network-accessible. Phase 2's goal: turn that single binary into something that can run as 
\f1\b 6 independent processes
\f2\b0 , where a client can hit 
\f3\i any
\f2\i0  one of them and the request gets transparently routed to whichever node actually owns that key.\
By the end of Phase 2, you will 
\f1\b not
\f2\b0  have replication, failure detection, or fault tolerance yet \'97 those are Phases 3, 4, and 5. Right now each key lives on exactly 
\f1\b one
\f2\b0  node. We're just building correct, deterministic routing.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 Step 0: Concepts you need before writing code\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Why not just 
\f4\fs26 hash(key) % 6
\f1\fs24 ?
\f2\b0  It works fine until a node dies or you add a node \'97 then almost 
\f3\i every
\f2\i0  key remaps to a different node, because the modulus changes. That's catastrophic for a live system (every node needs to re-fetch nearly all its data).\

\f1\b Consistent hashing
\f2\b0  fixes this: imagine a circle (the "ring") labeled with hash values from 
\f5\fs26 0
\f2\fs24  to 
\f5\fs26 2^64-1
\f2\fs24 . You hash each 
\f3\i node
\f2\i0  onto a point on this ring. You hash each 
\f3\i key
\f2\i0  onto a point on this ring too. A key belongs to the first node you encounter walking clockwise from the key's position. When one node leaves, only the keys that were mapped to 
\f3\i that
\f2\i0  node need to move (to the next node clockwise) \'97 everyone else's data stays put.\

\f1\b Why virtual nodes?
\f2\b0  If you only place each of your 6 physical nodes at one point on the ring, the ring gets divided into 6 wildly uneven arcs (random hashing doesn't give even spacing), so one node might get 40% of all keys. The trick: place each physical node at 
\f3\i many
\f2\i0  points on the ring (e.g., 150 points, each labeled 
\f5\fs26 "nodeAddress#0"
\f2\fs24 , 
\f5\fs26 "nodeAddress#1"
\f2\fs24 , ... 
\f5\fs26 "nodeAddress#149"
\f2\fs24 ). With enough virtual points, key distribution becomes statistically even, because you're averaging out the randomness over many points instead of relying on 6 lucky/unlucky hash values.\

\f1\b Design decisions for Phase 2 (keep it simple on purpose):
\f2\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Cluster membership is 
\f1\b static
\f2\b0  \'97 a plain text config file listing all node addresses, loaded once at startup. Dynamic membership (nodes joining/leaving live) comes in Phase 4 with gossip.\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Every node runs the 
\f3\i same
\f2\i0  binary and builds an 
\f3\i identical
\f2\i0  hash ring from the same config file, so every node agrees on who owns what.\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Routing is 
\f1\b one hop
\f2\b0 : client 
\f6 \uc0\u8594 
\f2  any node 
\f6 \uc0\u8594 
\f2  if that node isn't the owner, it forwards to the owner and relays the response back. This "any node can be a coordinator" pattern is exactly what Dynamo does, and it's what Phase 3 (replication) will build on top of.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
